\nproblem: |\n  Shell scripts use true as a no-op command that always succeeds. It appears in conditional\n  expressions, while loops (while true; do ...; done), and as a placeholder in pipelines.\n  The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gtrue, installed by\n  coreutils) in exit code behavior and output, verified by differential testing via\n  pkg/testutils under LC_ALL=C.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Exit with status 0 unconditionally, matching GNU true exit behavior.\n  - G2: Handle --help and --version flags, matching GNU true flag handling.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Exit status\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must exit with status 0 when invoked with no arguments.\n      - R1.2: Must exit with status 0 when invoked with any arguments that are not --help or --version. Arguments are not parsed, validated, or acted upon; they are silently ignored.\n      - R1.3: Must not read from stdin under any circumstances.\n      - R1.4: Must not write to stdout or stderr under normal operation (no arguments or unrecognized arguments).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag handling (--help, --version)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: When --help is the first argument, must print a usage message to stdout and exit 0. Remaining arguments after --help are ignored.\n      - R2.2: When --version is the first argument, must print version information to stdout and exit 0. Remaining arguments after --version are ignored.\n      - R2.3: Only the first argument is checked for --help and --version. If --help or --version appears in any position other than the first argument, it is ignored and true exits 0 silently.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Operand behavior\n    items:\n      - R3.1: All operands (non-flag arguments) are silently ignored. true does not distinguish between flags and operands; everything except --help and --version as the first argument is discarded.\n      - R3.2: The \"--\" end-of-options marker has no special meaning to true. It is ignored like any other argument.\n\n  R4:\n    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    items:\n      - R4.1: |\n          Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler, matching\n          the pattern used by other utilities in the project (see cmd/cat/main.go). This ensures\n          silent exit when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer in a pipeline.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must exit 0 in all cases except a write error during --help or --version output.\n      - R5.2: If a write error occurs while printing --help or --version output (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk), must exit 1.\n      - R5.3: \"Error messages for write failures must be written to stderr in the format: \\\"true: <error description>\\\".\"\n      - R5.4: Must not exit with any status other than 0 or 1.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/true does not implement any flags beyond --help and --version.\n  - cmd/true does not perform any computation or I/O beyond the optional help/version output.\n  - cmd/true does not read from stdin or process piped input.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/true; echo $? prints 0.\"\n  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/true foo bar --baz; echo $? prints 0.\"\n  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/true --help prints a usage message to stdout and exits 0.\"\n  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/true --version prints version information to stdout and exits 0.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gtrue passes with LC_ALL=C for no-args, arbitrary-args, and single-unrecognized-flag cases, comparing exit codes.\"\n  - \"AC6: Differential test verifies no stdout or stderr output when invoked without --help or --version, under LC_ALL=C.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/true compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n```\n\n## 2. road-map.yaml\n\n```yaml\nid: go-unix-utils-roadmap\ntitle: \"go-unix-utils Release Road Map\"\n\nreleases:\n  - version: \"00.0\"\n    name: \"Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure\"\n    status: spec_complete\n    description: |\n      Specify and ver