{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013nTtpZ22rVAxjrW4fmnwpN","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd021-false\n     2→title: \"cmd/false: Exit Unsuccessfully\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Shell scripts use false as a no-op command that always fails. It appears in conditional\n     6→  expressions, until loops (until false; do ...; done), and as a placeholder for unconditional\n     7→  failure in pipelines and control flow. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n     8→  reference binary (gfalse, installed by coreutils) in exit code behavior and output, verified\n     9→  by differential testing via pkg/testutils under LC_ALL=C.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Exit with status 1 unconditionally, matching GNU false exit behavior.\n    13→  - G2: Handle --help and --version flags, matching GNU false flag handling.\n    14→\n    15→requirements:\n    16→  R1:\n    17→    title: Exit status\n    18→    items:\n    19→      - R1.1: Must exit with status 1 when invoked with no arguments.\n    20→      - R1.2: Must exit with status 1 when invoked with any arguments that are not --help or --version. Arguments are not parsed, validated, or acted upon; they are silently ignored.\n    21→      - R1.3: Must not read from stdin under any circumstances.\n    22→      - R1.4: Must not write to stdout or stderr under normal operation (no arguments or unrecognized arguments).\n    23→\n    24→  R2:\n    25→    title: Flag handling (--help, --version)\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R2.1: When --help is the first argument, must print a usage message to stdout and exit 0. Remaining arguments after --help are ignored.\n    28→      - R2.2: When --version is the first argument, must print version information to stdout and exit 0. Remaining arguments after --version are ignored.\n    29→      - R2.3: Only the first argument is checked for --help and --version. If --help or --version appears in any position other than the first argument, it is ignored and false exits 1 silently.\n    30→\n    31→  R3:\n    32→    title: Operand behavior\n    33→    items:\n    34→      - R3.1: All operands (non-flag arguments) are silently ignored. false does not distinguish between flags and operands; everything except --help and --version as the first argument is discarded.\n    35→      - R3.2: The \"--\" end-of-options marker has no special meaning to false. It is ignored like any other argument.\n    36→\n    37→  R4:\n    38→    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    39→    items:\n    40→      - R4.1: |\n    41→          Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler, matching\n    42→          the pattern used by other utilities in the project (see cmd/cat/main.go). This ensures\n    43→          silent exit when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer in a pipeline.\n    44→\n    45→  R5:\n    46→    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    47→    items:\n    48→      - R5.1: Must exit 1 in all cases except --help or --version output (which exit 0), or a write error during --help or --version output.\n    49→      - R5.2: If a write error occurs while printing --help or --version output (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk), must exit 1.\n    50→      - R5.3: \"Error messages for write failures must be written to stderr in the format: \\\"false: <error description>\\\".\"\n    51→      - R5.4: Must not exit with any status other than 0 or 1.\n    52→\n    53→non_goals:\n    54→  - cmd/false does not implement any flags beyond --help and --version.\n    55→  - cmd/false does not perform any computation or I/O beyond the optional help/version output.\n    56→  - cmd/false does not read from stdin or process piped input.\n    57→\n    58→acceptance_criteria:\n    59→  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/false; echo $? prints 1.\"\n    60→  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/false foo bar --baz; echo $? prints 1.\"\n    61→  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/false --help prints a usage message to stdout and exits 0.\"\n    62→  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/false --version prints version information to stdout and exits 0.\"\n    63→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gfalse passes with LC_ALL=C for no-args, arbitrary-args, and single-unrecognized-flag cases, comparing exit codes.\"\n    64→  - \"AC6: Differential test verifies no stdout or stderr output when invoked without --help or --version, under LC_ALL=C.\"\n    65→  - \"AC7: cmd/false compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    66→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b8e2752-e0d3-4adb-aeb5-99f03f790516","uuid":"353e372b-72f2-4a2d-8818-f11af694cd7a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/203/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-false.yaml","content":"id: prd021-false\ntitle: \"cmd/false: Exit Unsuccessfully\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Shell scripts use false as a no-op command that always fails. It appears in conditional\n  expressions, until loops (until false; do ...; done), and as a placeholder for unconditional\n  failure in pipelines and control flow. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gfalse, installed by coreutils) in exit code behavior and output, verified\n  by differential testing via pkg/testutils under LC_ALL=C.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Exit with status 1 unconditionally, matching GNU false exit behavior.\n  - G2: Handle --help and --version flags, matching GNU false flag handling.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Exit status\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must exit with status 1 when invoked with no arguments.\n      - R1.2: Must exit with status 1 when invoked with any arguments that are not --help or --version. Arguments are not parsed, validated, or acted upon; they are silently ignored.\n      - R1.3: Must not read from stdin under any circumstances.\n      - R1.4: Must not write to stdout or stderr under normal operation (no arguments or unrecognized arguments).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag handling (--help, --version)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: When --help is the first argument, must print a usage message to stdout and exit 0. Remaining arguments after --help are ignored.\n      - R2.2: When --version is the first argument, must print version information to stdout and exit 0. Remaining arguments after --version are ignored.\n      - R2.3: Only the first argument is checked for --help and --version. If --help or --version appears in any position other than the first argument, it is ignored and false exits 1 silently.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Operand behavior\n    items:\n      - R3.1: All operands (non-flag arguments) are silently ignored. false does not distinguish between flags and operands; everything except --help and --version as the first argument is discarded.\n      - R3.2: The \"--\" end-of-options marker has no special meaning to false. It is ignored like any other argument.\n\n  R4:\n    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    items:\n      - R4.1: |\n          Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler, matching\n          the pattern used by other utilities in the project (see cmd/cat/main.go). This ensures\n          silent exit when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer in a pipeline.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must exit 1 in all cases except --help or --version output (which exit 0), or a write error during --help or --version output.\n      - R5.2: If a write error occurs while printing --help or --version output (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk), must exit 1.\n      - R5.3: \"Error messages for write failures must be written to stderr in the format: \\\"false: <error description>\\\".\"\n      - R5.4: Must not exit with any status other than 0 or 1.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/false does not implement any flags beyond --help and --version.\n  - cmd/false does not perform any computation or I/O beyond the optional help/version output.\n  - cmd/false does not read from stdin or process piped input.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/false; echo $? prints 1.\"\n  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/false foo bar --baz; echo $? prints 1.\"\n  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/false --help prints a usage message to stdout and exits 0.\"\n  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/false --version prints version information to stdout and exits 0.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gfalse passes with LC_ALL=C for no-args, arbitrary-args, and single-unrecognized-flag cases, comparing exit codes.\"\n  - \"AC6: Differential test verifies no stdout or stderr output when invoked without --help or --version, under LC_ALL=C.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/false compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":66,"startLine":1,"totalLines":66}}}
